# 1. CW-001 — Statistical experiments / Blackwell / Le Cam / order completion

## Erik Torgersen — *Comparison of Statistical Experiments* (1991)
Role in lab:
- historical/authoritative map of comparison theory;
- generalized decision rules and transitions;
- vector lattices, L/M-spaces, generalized experiments;
- deficiencies/randomization criterion;
- experiment functionals and conical measures;
- majorization/dilations;
- local comparison/Fisher-order material.

Claims already extracted in the lab:
- ordinary randomizations are inadequate in full generality; Le Cam transitions repair this;
- generalized variables/experiments are introduced to handle weak-compactness pathologies;
- representable experiment functionals and conical-measure representations are classical;
- deficiency has multiple equivalent decision-risk/randomization interpretations;
- products/mixtures and finite-parameter representations are classical.

Important ownership rule:
Do NOT claim "generalized experiment completion" as new. The unresolved CWR question is the precise relation between:
(1) MacNeille order completion,
(2) Le Cam generalized experiments/transitions,
(3) deficiency/topological completion.

## Lucien Le Cam — “Sufficiency and Approximate Sufficiency” (1964)
Role:
- primary source for transitions/approximate sufficiency/Riesz-lattice approach.
Previously established:
- Le Cam comparison theory enlarges randomizations to transitions;
- later sources summarize L-space / M-space structure.

Ownership rule:
The lab must distinguish *adding objects/cuts* (MacNeille) from *enlarging admissible morphisms/transitions* (Le Cam).

## Bertschinger & Rauh (2014), Blackwell order non-lattice paper
Role:
- explicit finite-state non-lattice witness.
Claims already verified:
- binary-state Blackwell order is a lattice;
- for more than two states, meets/joins fail in general;
- their 3-state example has a zonotope intersection with triangular faces and hence no representing channel.
CWR use:
- explicit nonprincipal MacNeille cut;
- exact representability test for completed common-information objects.

## Dedekind–MacNeille completion references
Role:
- order-theoretic engine.
Established:
\[
DM(P)=\{A\subseteq P:A=L(U(A))\}
\]
with embedding \(x\mapsto\downarrow x\);
preserves existing meets/joins and is the minimal complete-lattice completion in the standard sense.
CWR result:
for Blackwell classes,
\[
j(E_1)\wedge\cdots\wedge j(E_p)=\bigcap_i\downarrow E_i,
\]
and a genuine meet exists iff that cut is principal.

## Justin Farmer (2026), “Geometric Characterization of the Blackwell Order”
Role:
- high-priority contemporary collision.
What was actually known:
- abstract mentions lower contour sets and “completions” for two/three states.
What was NOT established:
- the exact theorem/construction could not be audited from the blocked full text.
Rule:
Do not infer Farmer’s exact construction from the abstract.

