# 4. Probabilistic automata / synchronization / undecidability

## Rabin (1963), probabilistic automata
Role:
- foundational probabilistic automata;
- cut points / isolated cut points;
- “actual automata” (strictly positive transition probabilities);
- stability/definiteness phenomena.
CWR interpretation:
positivity alone is not the decisive boundary; isolation/margin matters.

## Gimbert–Oualhadj
Role:
- value-1 problem undecidability.
CWR exact reduction:
designated-pair TV synchronization value
\[
v_{\rm pair}=1-\operatorname{val}(\mathcal A)
\]
for the constructed gadget.
Consequence:
pair synchronization to zero is undecidable in general.

## Classical PFA cut-point emptiness
Role:
- fixed-threshold undecidability (e.g. cutpoint \(1/2\)).
CWR consequence:
fixed-accuracy designated-pair TV synchronization is undecidable;
therefore no recursive general witness-length/reset bound.

## Fijalkow–Gimbert–Kelmendi–Oualhadj — leaktight automata / Markov monoid
Role:
- leaktight class;
- extended Markov monoid;
- leak witnesses;
- value-1 decidability/PSPACE result.
CWR interpretation:
a leak is a recurrent support/limit Booleanization defect:
finite-stage positivity persists but limiting probability vanishes.
Exact CWR deduction:
finite strictly-positive PFA families are leaktight because every product entry has a uniform positive floor.

## Profinite / prostochastic line
Role:
