# 5. Rote 2025 — central zero-margin collision

## Günter Rote (MFCS 2025), undecidability for positive/doubly-stochastic PFAs
This is one of the most important modern collisions in the current lab.

Claims already used:
- undecidability survives for small positive doubly-stochastic PFAs at the stationary cutpoint;
- a 7-state positive doubly-stochastic construction with cutpoint \(1/7\);
- fixed positive doubly-stochastic generators with only initial distribution varying in the stronger fixed-system form;
- separate binary positive constructions;
- positivity transformation of the form
  \[
  D_i=(1-\alpha)J+\alpha C_i
  \]
  preserving a signed computational signal multiplied by \(\alpha^{|w|}\).

CWR deductions:
- stationary-cutpoint singularity;
- vanishing-signal computation;
- leaktightness does not prevent zero-margin undecidability;
- trivial support + exponential mixing can coexist with undecidable transient sign reachability;
- one fixed system can be globally mixing while its basin-side exact transient predicate is undecidable.

Ownership:
Rote owns the core PFA undecidability and mixing-shell mechanism.
CWR may claim only exact deductions/reformulations after collision audit.

